  "query_preview": "..Two waves of energy can be noted on satellite\nanalysis this afternoon; one across northeast Utah/ southwest\nWyoming and one across southwestern Utah. For the northern area,\nthis shortwave is fairly worked over, with stratiform rain and\nisolated thunderstorms translating through the Uinta Mountains and\nUinta County, WY. The southern shortwave, however, continues to\nproduce a concentrated area of strong showers and thunderstorms as\nit continues to march northward. A moisture rich near-surface\nenvironment and stout synoptic support for lift will continue to\nsupport a threat of flash flooding under this area of showers and\nthunderstorms. This area of storms is expected to track northeast\nthrough the remainder of the evening, with the eastern side of the\ndisturbance looking like the primary focus area for stronger\nconvection. As the northeastern Utah wave continues its track \nnortheast as well, northern Utah will then move out of the\nsubsident wake which will assist with development of showers and\nthunderstorms across Utah's West Desert. Isolated storms are\nexpected to progress into the Wasatch Front through the evening,\nmainly the northern portions, and bring potential for strong\noutflow winds in excess of 40 mph.\n\nBy Thursday, a much drier air mass will build into the area and\ncut off nearly all chances for showers and thunderstorms across\nthe forecast area. Locations which may continue to see isolated\nshower and thunderstorm activity will be the Uinta Mountains,\nwhere high elevation surface heating may tap into high-based\ninstability associated with the departing mid-level moisture.\nOtherwise, anticipate a cumulus field to develop over the high\nterrain through the afternoon hours. There's generally a low\nchance (10% or less) that these clouds develop into showers,\nbut if it's going to happen, it'll happen over the central Utah\nmountains. That said, there will be a much lower chance for heavy\nrainfall and flash flooding. By Friday, moisture will be hard to\nfind over the area as surface relative humidity falls into the\nsingle digits to low-teens across the entire area alongside a near\nzero chance for showers.\n\nIn addition to the drier air mass, a significant warming trend is\nexpected across the entirety of the forecast area. Each day from\nThursday through Saturday, daytime highs are expected to increase\nby 3-5 degrees with overnight low temperatures following suit. By\nFriday, temperatures along the Wasatch Front will climb back to\nthe triple digit mark with overnight lows only falling into the\nlow- to mid-70s. For the lower elevations of southern Utah (lower\nWashington County/ Glen Canyon area), high temperatures will bump\ninto the low-110s with overnight lows only dropping into the\nupper-70s to low-80s. As such, a litany of heat headlines \n(extreme heat watches, warnings, and heat advisories) have been \nissued and will go into effect starting at noon on Friday. \n\nAn active northern jet stream over the PacNW and northern Rockies\nregion late in the weekend is expected to help bring an increase\nin southwest winds across a portion of Utah. Pairing with the very\nhot and very dry conditions, these increasingly breezy southwest\nwinds will bring elevated to near-critical fire weather\nconditions across southwest, west-central, and northeast Utah on\nSunday and Monday afternoon. A dry cold front is anticipated to\nmove into northern Utah and southwest Wyoming Sunday night into\nMonday, bringing at least some relief from heat. If enough\nnorthwest flow develops in the wake of this potential cold front,\nwe may begin to see impacts from wildfire smoke originating the\nColumbia Basin as we head into the first half of next week.",
